Project Context: JobFinder SPA
1. Project Overview
JobFinder is a job search application that allows candidates to browse international job offers via public APIs. The project is a Frontend-only Single Page Application (SPA) using Angular. There is no custom backend; JSON Server is used to simulate a REST API and persist data for users, favorites, and applications.

2. Technical Requirements
Framework: Angular 17+ (Standalone or Module-based).

State Management: NgRx is mandatory for managing the "Favorites" functionality.

Data Persistence: JSON Server (db.json) for users, favorites, and applications.

Authentication: Simulated auth storing user objects (minus passwords) in sessionStorage or localStorage.

Styling: Responsive design using Bootstrap or Tailwind CSS.

Key Features: RxJS Observables, Reactive Forms, Guards (AuthGuard), Resolvers, and Lazy Loading.

3. Data Schemas
3.1. External API: The Muse
Endpoint: https://www.themuse.com/api/public/jobs Job Object Structure:

JSON

{
  "id": "string",
  "name": "string", // Job Title
  "contents": "string", // HTML Description
  "publication_date": "YYYY-MM-DD",
  "locations": [{ "name": "string" }],
  "categories": [{ "name": "string" }],
  "levels": [{ "name": "string" }],
  "company": { "name": "string" },
  "refs": { "landing_page": "string" } // Link to full offer
}
3.2. Local Storage (JSON Server db.json)
users: Stores id, firstName, lastName, email, and password.

favoritesOffers: Stores id, userId, offerId, title, company, and location.

applications: Stores tracking info including status (en_attente, accepté, refusé) and notes.

4. Business Logic & Rules
Search Logic: Filters by Keyword (Title only) and Location. Results must be sorted by publication_date (Newest first).

Auth Rules: Search is public; Favorites and Applications are restricted to authenticated users via authGuard.

Favorites Logic (NgRx): * A user cannot add the same offer twice.

The UI must show a visual indicator if an offer is already in favorites.

Application Tracking: Users manually update statuses and can add optional personal notes.

5. Components Architecture
Each page must consist of at least two components (Parent/Child or Siblings).

Loading indicators must be displayed during API calls.

HTTP Interceptors should be used for centralized error handling or API key management.